Give the two generated headers a content check instead of a blind rewrite.

WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT ALMOST DID NOT.  R4-3, 2026-09-02.  This patch was
written first, against a diagnosis that turned out to be wrong, and it was very
nearly dropped.  `notes/dev-loop.md` §5.2 had measured that `include/linux/
bounds.h` and `include/asm-rlx/asm-offsets.h` are rewritten with a fresh mtime
and byte-identical content on every build, that `bounds.h` is named in 566 of
731 `.*.o.cmd` files, and concluded that this is what forces the 599-object
rebuild.  量 2026-09-02, with this patch applied to a staged tree and nothing
else changed: both mtimes stop moving, `CHK` goes 4 -> 6, and the rebuild is
**still 599 objects**.  The real cause was `arg-check` and it is `0004`.

WHAT KEPT IT.  With `0004` in place the header rewrite stops being a symptom and
becomes reachable again on its own, and it is expensive when it fires.  量, one
tree, one variable, 2026-09-02:

    touch kernel/bounds.c, then make      with this patch:    3 CC,  6.9 s
                                          without it:       573 CC, 26.3 s

`kernel/bounds.s` is regenerated whenever `kernel/bounds.c` or a header it
includes changes, and whenever the top-level `Kbuild` itself is touched -- and
`$(call cmd,bounds)` then rewrites `bounds.h` unconditionally, with identical
content, taking 572 objects with it.  So the value of this patch is not the
no-op case, where it is worth nothing; it is that one edit to a core header
costs 3 compilations instead of 573.

WHAT IT CHANGES.  `$(call cmd,bounds)` -> `$(call filechk,bounds)` and the same
for `offsets`, with the two `cmd_` bodies becoming `filechk_` bodies: the
`> $@` redirect and the `(set -e; …)` wrapper come off because `filechk`
supplies both, and `sed -ne $(sed-y) $<` becomes `sed -ne $(sed-y)` because
`filechk` pipes `$<` in on stdin.

WHOSE CHANGE IT IS.  Mainline's.  量: this drop's top-level `Kbuild` is sha256
`c1065aab0da23578` and mainline v2.6.30's is the same, in all three
`linux-2.6.30` trees on disk -- Realtek did not write this rule and did not
touch it.  Current mainline replaced both call sites with `filechk` for exactly
this reason, and `filechk` is already in this tree, byte-identical to mainline's
(`scripts/Kbuild.include:49`), documented as *"If they are equal no change, and
no timestamp update"*.  Nothing new is introduced.

WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE.  Not the `.s` rules, not `always`, not `targets`, not
the `FORCE` prerequisites.  `kernel/bounds.s` and `asm-offsets.s` are still
regenerated by `if_changed_dep`; what changes is only whether the resulting
HEADER is rewritten when its content did not move.  And not one byte of the
product: the generated headers' sha256 is the same before and after
(`bd0652b2b7b1a641`, `f7575686e3719433`).

SCOPE.  Host build tooling only.  It changes when kbuild rewrites a generated
header, never what the header says.

--- a/Kbuild
+++ b/Kbuild
@@ -13,10 +13,8 @@
 always  := $(bounds-file)
 targets := $(bounds-file) kernel/bounds.s
 
-quiet_cmd_bounds = GEN     $@
-define cmd_bounds
-	(set -e; \
-	 echo "#ifndef __LINUX_BOUNDS_H__"; \
+define filechk_bounds
+	(echo "#ifndef __LINUX_BOUNDS_H__"; \
 	 echo "#define __LINUX_BOUNDS_H__"; \
 	 echo "/*"; \
 	 echo " * DO NOT MODIFY."; \
@@ -25,9 +23,9 @@
 	 echo " *"; \
 	 echo " */"; \
 	 echo ""; \
-	 sed -ne $(sed-y) $<; \
+	 sed -ne $(sed-y); \
 	 echo ""; \
-	 echo "#endif" ) > $@
+	 echo "#endif" )
 endef
 
 # We use internal kbuild rules to avoid the "is up to date" message from make
@@ -37,7 +35,7 @@
 
 $(obj)/$(bounds-file): kernel/bounds.s Kbuild
 	$(Q)mkdir -p $(dir $@)
-	$(call cmd,bounds)
+	$(call filechk,bounds)
 
 #####
 # 2) Generate asm-offsets.h
@@ -57,10 +55,8 @@
 	s:->::; p;}"
 endef
 
-quiet_cmd_offsets = GEN     $@
-define cmd_offsets
-	(set -e; \
-	 echo "#ifndef __ASM_OFFSETS_H__"; \
+define filechk_offsets
+	(echo "#ifndef __ASM_OFFSETS_H__"; \
 	 echo "#define __ASM_OFFSETS_H__"; \
 	 echo "/*"; \
 	 echo " * DO NOT MODIFY."; \
@@ -69,9 +65,9 @@
 	 echo " *"; \
 	 echo " */"; \
 	 echo ""; \
-	 sed -ne $(sed-y) $<; \
+	 sed -ne $(sed-y); \
 	 echo ""; \
-	 echo "#endif" ) > $@
+	 echo "#endif" )
 endef
 
 # We use internal kbuild rules to avoid the "is up to date" message from make
@@ -81,7 +77,7 @@
 	$(call if_changed_dep,cc_s_c)
 
 $(obj)/$(offsets-file): arch/$(SRCARCH)/kernel/asm-offsets.s Kbuild
-	$(call cmd,offsets)
+	$(call filechk,offsets)
 
